Dead-code elimination 6.2 Techniques




 DEFINITION

 Dead-code elimination: remove statements that never execute (unreachable) or whose result is never used (dead stores) and that have no side effects.



BEFORE AFTER


 i = 0; i = 0;
 if (i == 1) { /* if removed: condition is false */
 a = x + 5;
 }


BEFORE AFTER


 global = 1; /* dead store */ global = 2;
 global = 2; return;
 return;
 global = 3; /* unreachable */



 NOTE

 After removing dead code, new simplifications may appear.


